<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>API_HOST</key>
	<string>$(API_HOST)</string>
	<key>API_PATH</key>
	<string>$(API_PATH)</string>
	<key>API_PATH_M</key>
	<string>$(API_PATH_M)</string>
	<key>API_PROTOCOL</key>
	<string>$(API_PROTOCOL)</string>
	<!-- Apple's own string, verbatim out of the shared cache:
	     "LSSupportsGameMode key not found in Info.plist. This key will become
	      required to be eligible for Game Mode in a future OS release."
	     Declared here rather than as a build setting because Xcode has no
	     INFOPLIST_KEY for it yet. -->
	<!-- Off, on purpose, on Mathias's decision of 2026-09-01. Game Mode
	     prioritises the frontmost declared game and lowers everything else;
	     the game itself runs under wine, which gamepolicyd does not see, so
	     the only thing Game Mode could ever have prioritised here is this
	     launcher -- at the game's expense. The key stays, set to false, so
	     the intent is stated rather than inferred from its absence.

	     What happened after, reported by Mathias on 2026-09-03: about 30 fps
	     more, and Mortal Shell in particular much steadier. Worth writing
	     down, and worth not over-reading. The change that removed this
	     shipped a second one beside it -- the launcher also lets go of the
	     controller while a game runs -- so neither is separated from the
	     other by this evidence. And it sits oddly with what was measured
	     before removing: gamepolicyd had logged nothing in 24 hours, which
	     was taken to mean Game Mode was not engaging at all. A gain that
	     size says either that silence was not evidence of inaction, or the
	     gain belongs to the controller change. Separating them is one build
	     each way, and nobody has spent a session on it. -->
	<key>LSSupportsGameMode</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
